# Saving a response to disk via a "# @download" annotation -- a marker
# this plugin recognizes, not httpfly itself (httpfly's own equivalent is
# a plain "-download F" flag on "run", which only ever applies to a single
# selected request -- it has no per-request annotation of its own). This
# plugin scans the buffer for "# @download" and, when present on a
# request, wires up "-download" automatically before sending -- no
# separate command, no prompt.
#
# Works for any response, not just binary content -- DownloadJson below
# saves a JSON body just as well as DownloadAuto/DownloadNamed save a PNG
# (a post-request script + response.body, see 6_save_response.http, is
# just as byte-accurate for binary content in httpfly -- Lua strings are
# byte arrays, not decoded Unicode text). The advantage here is
# convenience: no script to write, and it works the same whether the
# request is sent via :HttpSend or as part of :HttpSendAll.
#
# Saved under .httpfly/downloads/ next to this file (same place
# .httpfly/history/ and .httpfly/state.json already live -- one
# .gitignore entry covers all of it). Bare "# @download" picks a filename
# from the URL's last path segment -- httpbin's /image/png has no real
# filename in its path (the segment is literally "png"), so DownloadAuto
# below saves as "png". Unlike a browser (or the old httpyac-backed
# version of this plugin), there's no way to name the file from the
# *response* (Content-Disposition, a content-type-guessed extension):
# httpfly needs the destination path upfront, before the request is even
# sent. "# @download <name>" (see DownloadNamed/DownloadJson below)
# overrides it explicitly -- prefer that when the URL alone won't give you
# a sensible name.
#
# Works the same whether sent with :HttpSend (just this request) or
# :HttpSendAll (every request in the file, download-annotated or not).
#
# Run `make httpbin-up` from the repo root first (requires Docker).

###
# @name DownloadAuto
# @download
GET http://localhost:8080/image/png HTTP/1.1

###
# @name DownloadNamed
# @download my-photo.png
GET http://localhost:8080/image/png HTTP/1.1

###
# @name DownloadJson
# @download report.json
GET http://localhost:8080/json HTTP/1.1
